    "lines": [
      {
        "scene": "hook",
        "text": "Why does Gambia sit inside Senegal, like a worm inside an apple?",
        "start": 0.3,
        "seconds": 3.12,
        "window": 3.8,
        "fill": 0.821
      },
      {
        "scene": "promise",
        "text": "Gambia is a real country, with its own flag and its own money, and Senegal wraps around it on every side but the sea.",
        "start": 4.1,
        "seconds": 5.25,
        "window": 5.93,
        "fill": 0.885
      },
      {
        "scene": "long",
        "text": "From the coast, it runs almost 480 km inland, yet at its widest it is only about 50 km across.",
        "start": 10.03,
        "seconds": 5.69,
        "window": 6.37,
        "fill": 0.893
      },
      {
        "scene": "border",
        "text": "Senegal is its only neighbour, and their border winds on for 749 km, through villages, farms and forest.",
        "start": 16.4,
        "seconds": 5.7,
        "window": 6.3,
        "fill": 0.905
      },
      {
        "scene": "river",
        "text": "So how did that happen? The answer is the river. In the age of sailing ships, it was a highway deep into Africa, and Britain held its mouth.",
        "start": 22.7,
        "seconds": 6.05,
        "window": 6.7,
        "fill": 0.903
      },
      {
        "scene": "france",
        "text": "France held the land all around it, the colony that became Senegal.",
        "start": 29.4,
        "seconds": 2.96,
        "window": 3.6,
        "fill": 0.822
      },
      {
        "scene": "deal",
        "text": "So in 1889, the two empires made a deal. Britain kept the river, and land about 10 km back from its banks.",
        "start": 33.0,
        "seconds": 5.49,
        "window": 6.17,
        "fill": 0.89
      },
      {
        "scene": "freedom",
        "text": "France took everything else. In 1965, Gambia became independent, inside exactly the same line.",
        "start": 39.17,
        "seconds": 5.04,
        "window": 5.7,
        "fill": 0.884
      },
      {
        "scene": "union",
        "text": "So did anyone ever fix it? In 1982, the two countries even joined as one, Senegambia. By 1989, they had split again.",
        "start": 44.87,
        "seconds": 6.34,
        "window": 7.0,
        "fill": 0.906
      },
      {
        "scene": "payoff",
        "text": "So a line drawn for river boats, about 10 km from the water, is still where a whole country begins and ends.",
        "start": 51.87,
        "seconds": 5.28,
        "window": 5.93,
        "fill": 0.89
      },
      {
        "scene": "loop",
        "text": "Gambia or Lesotho: which border is stranger? And if a friend ever asks you,",
        "start": 57.8,
        "seconds": 3.55,
        "window": 4.2,
        "fill": 0.845
      }
    ]
  },
